#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116233} :activity/id #uuid "e0acd4c8-93c4-5865-ac51-9faa700a780f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048589 :topic/id #uuid "abfa8789-e071-589e-9ce5-37b83eed3011" :topic/math-academy-id 1633} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116536 :tutorial/content "Suppose the random variable $X\\sim B(n,p)$ follows a binomial distribution.\n\nThere are certain situations where working with binomial random variables becomes difficult. For example, when the parameter $n$ is large, computing exact probabilities can become problematic due to the large numbers involved.\n\nUnder certain conditions, we can approximate the *discrete* random variable $X$ using a *continuous normal* random variable $Y$, given by\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are calculated using the formulas for the mean and variance of the binomial random variable $X$:\n\n$$\n\\mu = np, \\qquad \\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nTo determine whether a given binomial distribution can be approximated using a normal distribution, we use the following rule of thumb:\n\n- $np > 5$, and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$.\n\nTo get a feel for how this works, let's consider a few examples of binomial distributions and their corresponding normal approximations:\n\n- First, consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.2$. In this case, it's straightforward to show that\n$\\mu = {\\color{blue}{1}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{0.8}}$,\nand\n$np = 1 < 5,\\qquad n(1-p) = 4 < 5$.\nSince $np < 5$, the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{0.8}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.2)$. The diagram below compares the two distributions. The yellow rectangles correspond to the values of the PMF of $B(5, 0.2)$, and the smooth blue curve is the PDF of the corresponding normal distribution.![](images/6a/6a67b3ad65bfedcd25b787ff15e72c575da1215ae47502ff11f97d40de407560.png)\n- Next, we consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.5$. In this case, it's straightforward to show that\n$\\mu = {\\color{blue}{2.5}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{1.25}}$,\nand\n$np = n(1-p) = 2.5 < 5$.\nSince $np = n(1-p) < 5$, the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{2.5}},{\\color{red}{1.25}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.5)$.![](images/0a/0a8f2d9ccd78ee885ea3bb8f2f00705f69c520f8ec0588411865399b6ecef046.png)\n- Finally, we consider the case where $n=20$ and $p=0.5$. In this case, it's straightforward to show that\n$\\mu = {\\color{blue}{10}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{5}}$,\nand\n$np = n(1-p) = 10 > 5$.\nSince $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$, the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{10}},{\\color{red}{5}})$ *is* a good approximation to $B(20, 0.5)$.![](images/9e/9efc7df7c423ed2d8d6cc70ef820de3cb235f644567a3fec0d0a0e501042e305.png)\n\nThe fact that we can approximate binomial random variables as normal when $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ is a consequence of the so-called **central limit theorem** (or CLT). We'll discuss this in more detail at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d16ee6a-2c83-55f2-a37f-2b5264587984" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4970 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f1b69042-4e4f-5d93-b2e6-d2792a279297" :step/math-academy-id 30951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116234}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115144 :question/id #uuid "bb96e3e7-5fc4-58ed-a666-41e990e1b96d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8285"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f5f60f5-007d-5b1c-b3bc-f352175dcc91" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049632}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115991}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Normal Approximation is Appropriate"} :step/id #uuid "029cc3c0-aac1-5ab3-b3dc-fe7cf275acc3" :step/math-academy-id 30952 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116266}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115145 :question/id #uuid "4c4e12c4-1593-500d-873d-863b4a825e91" :question/math-academy-id "e-8286"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050425}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115992}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability"} :step/id #uuid "0681c605-1849-50ee-9d50-5be4f585dc4d" :step/math-academy-id 30953 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115146 :question/id #uuid "9019ee77-a971-598c-b5b6-4ef903fd5029" :question/math-academy-id "e-8287"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410433}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "a44f609d-29bb-5f64-b769-1c94ec6780fe" :step/math-academy-id 30954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116266 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116542 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random variable $X \\sim B(50,0.6)$. Let's estimate the value of\n\n$$\nP(X=33)\n$$\n\nusing the normal approximation.\n\nGiven a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, if\n\n- $np > 5$, and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$,\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nthat is,\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p))\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nnp &= 50 \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 30 \\\\\n&> 5✓ \\\\\nn(1 - p) &= 50 \\cdot (1 - 0.6) \\\\\n&= 20 \\\\\n&> 5.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = 50\\cdot0.6 = 30\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 50\\cdot0.6\\cdot(1-0.6)=12\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12)\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ is discrete and $Y$ is continuous, we need to apply a continuity correction. The required continuity correction in this case is\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(32.5 \\le Y \\le 33.5) &= P(\\frac{32.5 - 30}{\\sqrt{12}} \\le Z \\le \\frac{33.5 - 30}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(0.72 \\le Z \\le 1.01) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 1.01) - P(Z < 0.72) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 1.01) - P(Z \\le 0.72) \\\\\n&= Φ(1.01) - Φ(0.72)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a table of values for the CDF of the standard normal distribution, we find that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.01) = 0.8438, \\qquad \\Phi(0.72) = 0.7642\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(32.5 \\le Y \\le 33.5) &= Φ(1.01) - Φ(0.72) \\\\\n&= 0.8438 - 0.7642 \\\\\n&= 0.0796\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx 0.0796\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "22b0d565-b5ca-5c37-9d8a-f8a2c8f83fce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5618 :tutorial/title "Using the Normal Approximation to the Binomial Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "41a4f348-1e37-5763-9563-f9ba63ffa00a" :step/math-academy-id 34580 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116267}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116543 :tutorial/content "In the last example, we approximated the binomial random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim B(50,0.6)\n$$\n\nusing a normal random variable\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12)\n$$\n\nand we arrived at the approximation\n\n$$\nP(X=33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5) \\approx 0.0796\n$$\n\nIf you were to use a software package to evaluate this approximation, you might get an answer like\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5) \\approx 0.0791\n$$\n\nNotice that there is a difference of around $0.0005$ between the two answers.\n\nThere is a reason for this discrepancy. When we calculated that\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5) \\approx 0.0796\n$$\n\nwe rounded our $z$-values two decimal places and then used a lookup table to find $\\Phi(z)$ for those rounded values. As a result, we introduced some small rounding errors.\n\nTypically, the rounding errors are small, so we will continue to work with a table of values. However, it's worth bearing this in mind if you're using a software package or calculator to compute $\\Phi(z)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "d460cc56-a34f-5495-874f-19d80c5ad3b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5619 :tutorial/title "A Note on Rounding Errors"} :step/id #uuid "88da9ae9-0589-5f70-ab03-1c92544b026c" :step/math-academy-id 34581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116235}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116546 :tutorial/content "According to the so-called **central limit theorem**, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) random variables, then\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is \"sufficiently large,\" and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2\n$$\n\nNow, if $X\\sim B(n,p)$, then $X$ can be thought of as a sum of $n$ I.I.D Bernoulli random variables\n\n$$\nX_i\\sim\\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)\n$$\n\nwhere the PMF of each $X_i$ is given by\n\n$$\n{p,\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1 \\\\ 1 - p,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2 = p(1-p)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting our values of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ into our normal approximation for $X$, we get\n\n$$\nX \\approx N\\left(np, np(1-p)\\right)\n$$\n\nThe \"sufficiently large\" condition on $n$ is taken care of by stipulating that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$:\n\n- When $p\\approx 0.5$, then $n\\approx 10$ is sufficiently large for the normal approximation to be applied.\n- However, if $p$ is closer to zero or one, then the distribution of $X$ is not symmetric, and consequently, we need $n$ to be much larger for the central limit theorem to be valid." :tutorial/id #uuid "9a338343-d2c2-54a6-98b5-5033bebeb652" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6514 :tutorial/title "The Normal Approximation as a Consequence of the Central Limit Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "b9f0c982-62ce-5f5d-9d4d-30e10da26092" :step/math-academy-id 39428}] :activity/title "Normal Approximations of Binomial Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115440}]}
